<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhengzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhengzhou is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> province in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, a major railway hub near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. Beneath the modern city lie the rammed-earth walls of an early Shang city of the Erligang period, about seven kilometres around, dating from about 1600 to 1400 BCE. It is one of the oldest walled cities known in China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhengzhou.jpg" alt="Zhengzhou" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 白诗棋 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhengzhou_CBDcity.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zhengzhou lies in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, on the southern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, at the eastern foothills of the Xiong'er Mountains where the river's valley widens into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> to the east and Xinxiang to the northeast. The city stands at the crossing of the north–south route skirting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taihang-mountains">Taihang Mountains</a> and the mountains of western Henan. Its prefecture covers 7,567 km2, of which the city centre occupies 709.69 km2. It lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-plain-china">Zhongyuan</a> region, an area inhabited continuously for over 4,000 years and regarded as one of the places where Chinese civilisation began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Several city ruins from the early Shang dynasty have been found in the Zhengzhou area, including candidate sites for the early Shang capitals Ao, Bo or Yan. Since 1950 excavations at Zhengzhou Shang City, near the historical centre of the modern city, have provided evidence of Shang settlement around 1600 BCE. About 20 km to the northwest, remains of large public buildings and a complex of small settlements have been found at a site that some identify as the Shang capital of Ao. Parts of the Shang city wall, built some 3,600 years ago, still stand in the downtown.</p><p>After the Shang the area remained an important regional city because of its position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. The settlement was first known as Guancheng, the city of the Guan family, who held it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>. It later became the seat of a prefecture under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>, and the character Zheng in its modern name refers to the Zheng state that once governed the region. In June 1938, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>, Nationalist forces breached the dikes at Huayuankou, north of Zhengzhou, to slow the Japanese advance. The flood inundated large areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, Anhui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> did not return to its original course until 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Zhengzhou is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, with a population of about 12.6 million in the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is a main national transport hub, and it has been a major industrial city of the People's Republic since 1949, with textiles as its staple industry. The Zhengzhou Commodity Exchange, approved by the State Council in October 1990, was the first futures exchange in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Zhengzhou Confucius Temple, first built in the Eastern Han dynasty, is among the oldest Confucian temples in China. About 90 km southwest, in Dengfeng, the Shaolin Monastery and its Pagoda Forest were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhengzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeologically, the early Shang is usually identified with the Erligang culture, whose walled city at <b>Zhengzhou</b>, enclosed by rammed-earth walls about seven kilometres long, and the walled town at Yanshi date from roughly 1600–1400 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Production of bronzes and other elite goods at Erlitou stopped at the end of Phase IV, at the same time as the Erligang city of <b>Zhengzhou</b> was established 85 km to the east.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It was trapezoidal in plan: the east and south walls each ran about 2,000 metres and the west wall 1,600 metres, enclosing 3.6 square kilometres, similar in scale to the inner city of the Shang city at <b>Zhengzhou</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sanxingdui-culture">Sanxingdui culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late period Xishan in central Henan, a settlement of 25 hectares near modern <b>Zhengzhou</b>, received the first wall of rammed earth in China, and the first walled town of the Yellow River valley appeared.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yangshao-culture">Yangshao culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
